to change shell for an user
>chsh
example :
[root@ip-172-31-7-169 ec2-user]# chsh
Changing shell for root.
New shell [/bin/bash]:/bin/ksh
shell changed

to change default shell of specific user
chsh user_name
ex: chsh ec2-user
Changing shell for ec2-user.
New shell [/bin/bash]: /bin/ksh
Shell changed.

you are currently logged in as ec2-user and you user bash shell and now you change default shell of ec2-user to ksh and  it will not automatically change the current shell to ksh, you need to automatically change the shell

when you switch the user also shell will not be changed to users default shell, same shell which you are using will be retained, you need to change current shell manually

<!-- here if you visit /etc/passwd the default shell for root will be changes to /bin/ksh -->

[root@ip-172-31-7-169 ec2-user]# echo $SHELL
/bin/bash
[root@ip-172-31-7-169 ec2-user]# chsh ec2-user
Changing shell for ec2-user.
New shell [/bin/bash]: /bin/ksh
Shell changed.
[root@ip-172-31-7-169 ec2-user]# echo $SHELL
/bin/bash

The value of $SHELL didn't change because the chsh command updates your future login settings in a system file, but it does not change the environment variables of your current, already-running terminal session.
chsh Writes to Disk (/etc/passwd)
The change is saved successfully, but it only acts as a blueprint for future logins.
The $SHELL environment variable is read-only populated once at the exact moment you first log into a terminal session.
• Modifying the file on disk via chsh does not dynamically update the active memory of a terminal that is already running.



