su - ec2-user switches your current terminal session to the ec2-user login shell, loading that user's environment variables, path, and home directory.
Dash (-): A login shell simulation. It initializes a clean environment as if ec2-user logged in directly via a login session (changing directory to /home/ec2-user and updating shell profile variables like $PATH).

if you exit, it will go to previous state and previous shell level



shell-scripting:
you dont need to compile script unlike other programming languages
it will be directly interpreted by OS

when you write a shell script inside a file, you need to tell which shell it should use to execute at its first line

#! /bin/bash

when you execute this .sh file it will be executed in bash shell

you should have execute permissions to that file
ls -l
chmod +x file_name

to execute a file without permissions, use
shell_name file_name.sh

bash script.sh

